# ez-local-gif — compose settings. Copy to .env (git-ignored); docker compose
# reads it from the project directory. Everything here is optional: with every
# line commented out you get the plain app and no AI sidecar.

# Profiles a plain `docker compose up -d` includes (comma list). The AI matte
# sidecar lives behind one of two profiles — start ONE, never both (they share
# the "matte" network alias):
#   matte      CPU sidecar   (duckautomata/ezlg-matte:<tag>)
#   matte-gpu  CUDA sidecar  (duckautomata/ezlg-matte:<tag>-cuda; nvidia-container-toolkit, driver ≥ 580).
#              It offers the GPU and the CPU: the user picks "Run on" in the Background card, so a
#              box with a GPU wants this profile even for CPU passes. Nothing stays loaded between
#              passes (self-test at start, then released; TTL 300 s; unloaded when the AI mode is left).
# Without this line pass it per command: docker compose --profile matte-gpu up -d
#COMPOSE_PROFILES=matte-gpu

# Sidecar image tag. compose.yaml pins the tag this checkout ships with; set a
# previous one here to roll the sidecar back (then `docker compose up -d`), or a
# newer one to try it before updating the app. Never "latest": the app folds the
# sidecar's weights into every result, so an unpinned sidecar changes outputs
# under your feet. A release tag exists on Docker Hub only once that release was
# tagged (`git tag -a vX.Y.Z && git push origin vX.Y.Z` — the image workflow
# publishes :vX.Y.Z and :vX.Y.Z-cuda on the tag push); a master build is
# published as its short SHA only, so set that here to run one:
#EZLG_MATTE_TAG=v0.5.0
#EZLG_MATTE_TAG=1a2b3c4
